          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong strict category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            together with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            (not merely
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %strong weak category
            is a category
            %em without
            such structure. Similarly, a
            %strong strict functor
            is one which preserves equality of objects:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(x) = F(y)
            if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"} large
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+categories"} small categories
            can be either strict or weak.)
          %p
            If we use
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set-theoretic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            for category theory, then by definition, a category comes with a set (or class) of objects, and therefore admits the structure of a strict category in a canonical way. Thus, in this case, it is possible to ignore the adjective “strict” in “strict category”, while a “strict functor” simply means a “non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} ana
            functor” (the usual default meaning of ‘functor’, but not necessarily the best concept).
          %p
            If we additionally assume the (possibly global)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to a canonical strict category, but every (ana-)functor is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/naturally+isomorphic+functors"} naturally isomorphic
            to a strict functor, so the notions have little to no mathematical content. But in other foundations, these facts may not be true.
          %p
            Moreover, even in set-theoretic foundations with global
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi AC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC
            %em make use of
            the equality relation on its objects. When taking the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophical
            position that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is fundamentally about weak categories; see the discussion at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            In order to formulate a foundation-independent definition, we make the following assumptions. Ignoring
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/size+issues"} size issues
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            consisting of weak
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            (some of which are
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            (some of which are
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphisms"} natural isomorphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            consisting of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Disc
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc\colon Set \to \Cat
            taking each set to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete category
            on that set. Note that we do not assume the existence of an “underlying set of objects” functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat\to Set
          %p
            In this context, a
            %strong strict category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            consists of
          %ul
            %li
              a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob(C)
              (to be thought of as the set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              of
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              a category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Wk
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Wk(C)
              (to be thought of as the underlying weak category of
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              an essentially surjective functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Disc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Wk
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cl(C)\colon Disc(Ob(C)) \to Wk(C)
              (to be thought of as taking each object of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              to its
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clique"} clique
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            A
            %strong strict functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            (both strict categories) consists of
          %ul
            %li
              a function
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob(F)\colon Ob(C) \to Ob(D)
            %li
              a functor
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Wk
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Wk
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Wk
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Wk(F)\colon Wk(C) \to Wk(D)
            %li
              a natural isomorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ;
                      %mi Wk
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Disc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ;
                      %mi cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cl(F)\colon cl(C) ; Wk(F) \to Disc(Ob(F)) ; cl(D)
          %p
            Two strict functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F, G\colon C \to D
            are
            %strong equal
            if
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob(F) = Ob(G)
              and
            %li
              there is a (necessarily unique) natural isomorphism from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Wk
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Wk(F)
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Wk
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Wk(G)
              modulo which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cl(F)
              equals
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cl(G)
          %p
            There is an obvious notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            that defines a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Str
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str Cat
            of strict categories and (equality classes of) strict functors. Note that this 1-category has a strictly associative and unital composition law, even if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            is only a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Str
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str Cat
            is not
            %em itself
            a strict category, unless the assumed category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is strict.) We have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ob
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Str
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob\colon Str Cat \to Set
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudofunctor"} pseudofunctor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Wk
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Str
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Wk\colon Str Cat \to Cat
          %p
            Finally, a
            %strong natural transformation
            between two strict functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is simply
          %ul
            %li
              a natural transformation from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Wk
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Wk(F)
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Wk
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Wk(G)
          %p
            (That is, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            between strict functors is automatically strict.) With these as 2-cells, we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            of strict categories, strict functors, and natural transformations.
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              An equivalent definition is that a strict category is an
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-category"} category
              whose underlying category of tight morphisms is discrete, while a strict functor is an
              = succeed "-functor." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
              = succeed "." do
                %strong special isomorphisms
          %h2#foundational_choices Foundational choices
          %p We now make the above schematic definition explicit in terms of various different foundational systems.
          %h3#set_theory_with_ac Set theory with AC
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set-theoretic
            foundations including the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            consisting of (say small)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Disc
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc\colon Set \to Cat
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            to the functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob\colon Cat \to Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Str
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str Cat
            is a subcategory of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd+cover"} Freyd cover
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
          %p
            In this case, the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Wk
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Str
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Wk\colon Str Cat \to Cat
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            which takes a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to the strict category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Disc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc(Ob(C)) \to C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences"} equivalences
            (but not isomorphisms) in the strict
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Str
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Str
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str Cat
            is equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            (as a bicategory).
          %p
            The right adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo →
                  %mi Str
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat \to Str Cat
            also has a further right adjoint defined as follows: given a strict category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
            be the category with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob(C)
            as its set of objects, and morphisms induced from those in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Wk
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Wk(C)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Str
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str Cat
          %p
            This analysis applies not only to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theories"} material set theories
            such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theories"} structural set theories
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
          %h3#set_theory_without_ac Set theory without AC
          %p
            In this case, it is usually best to define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            to be the bicategory of categories,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctors"} anafunctors
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob\colon Cat \to Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            admits the structure of a strict category in a canonical way (with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob(C)
            its set of objects), but not every strict category necessarily arises in this way, and not every morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to D
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            (i.e. anafunctor) is a strict functor relative to the canonical strict-category structures of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            If we instead defined
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            to consist only of non-ana functors, then everything would happen exactly as with AC, except that neither adjunction would be an equivalence.
          %h3#type_and_preset_theories_with_equality Type and preset theories with equality
          %p
            We include under this heading all theories in which the basic objects, generally called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} types
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presets"} presets
            (and sometimes even called ‘sets’), come with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            is a defined one. Generally, the issue is that types (or presets) do not admit
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+object"} quotients
            %strong set
            is defined to be a type equipped with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            called “equality” (sometimes called a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoid"} setoid
            in this context, especially if the types or presets are themselves confusingly called ‘sets’).
          %p
            In this case, it is natural to define a
            %em category
            to have only a type/preset of objects, but a set(oid) of morphisms between each pair of objects; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type-theoretic+definition+of+category"} type-theoretic definition of category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completely+presented+sets"} completely presented sets
            have this property.
          %p
            Again, it is true that every category can be made into a strict one in a canonical way, but that not every strict category arises in this way. This is a different sort of failure than in set theory without
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi AC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC
            (even for setoids). For instance, consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete category
            on the set of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+real+number"} Cauchy real number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi AC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC
          %p
            Note that we still have a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Disc
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc\colon Set \to Cat
            which regards an equivalence relation on a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as a groupoid with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as its type of objects. The resulting structure of strict categories behaves much like it does in set theory without AC.
          %h3#type_and_preset_theories_without_equality Type and preset theories without equality
          %p
            If types or presets do not come with a notion of equality, as in some preset theories and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR%2B%CE%B5"} SEAR+ε
            %em have
            an “underlying set of objects”, so a category cannot be made into a strict category in any canonical way. But defining sets as presets with equivalence relations as before, we still have
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Disc
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc\colon Set \to Cat
          %h3#homotopy_type_theory Homotopy type theory
          %p
            If
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            is treated as a theory of set-like objects, then it behaves as above, whether
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            %em set
            is now defined to be an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-set"} h-set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated+object"} 0-truncated
            type.
          %p
            Now the natural definition of (1-)category is something like a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Segal+space"} complete Segal space
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
            of the category. Now (even though identity types exist in HoTT), not every category has an underlying
            %em set
            of objects, so again a category cannot be made into a strict category in any canonical way. But again, we still have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Disc
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc\colon Set \to Cat
            and we can define strict categories.
          %h3#fibered_categories Fibered categories
          %p
            In
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+B%C3%A9nabou"} Jean Bénabou
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Fibered categories and the foundations of naive category theory
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Fibered+categories+and+the+foundations+of+naive+category+theory"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}
            is given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} fibered category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon \mathbf{F} \to \mathbf{B}
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+category"} indexed category
            for more motivation.) In this context, equality of objects is available precisely when the fibration is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+fibration"} split
          %p
            In particular, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}
            is locally small and has
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullbacks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal category
            object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}
            (in the traditional sense) gives rise to an honest functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B} (-, C)\colon \mathbf{B}^{op} \to Cat
            and so a split fibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_\mathbf{B} \mathbf{B} (-, C) \to \mathbf{B}
            by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"} Grothendieck construction
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              Any model
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, \in_V)
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
              is a strict category, with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              being the set of objects of the strict category, and given elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in V
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} hom
                      %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{hom}_V(a, b)
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+set"} function set
              between the sets
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi V
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} and
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi V
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x \in V \vert x \in_V a\} \qquad \mathrm{and} \qquad \{x \in V \vert x \in_V b\}
          %p
            There are some at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-category"} M-category
          %h2#strict_higher_categories Strict higher categories
          %p
            The notion of strict category is related to the notion of strictness in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %p
            For instance, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            must have strict categories as its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-categories"} hom-categories
            in order to write down the definition of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-functors"} strict 2-functors
            %strong
              strict
              = succeed "-natural" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-natural+transformation"} 2-natural transformation
            that preserves equality (that is, if two objects are equal, then so are the corresponding components of the natural transformation). (All
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modifications"} modifications
            between strict transformations are automatically strict … until we get to
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
          %p
            On the other hand, the underlying 1-category of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            (its objects and 1-morphisms) need not be a strict 1-category. For instance, this is the case for the strict 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Str
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str Cat
            defined in a structural set theory, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
            = succeed "-category\u2019" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-category\u2019," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-strict," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "-strict," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %h2#references References
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            (via
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+categories"} univalent categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+identity+principle"} structure identity principle
            are discussed in chapter 10 of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benedikt+Ahrens"}> Benedikt Ahrens
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paige+Randall+North"} Paige Randall North
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"} Michael Shulman
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dimitris+Tsementzis"} Dimitris Tsementzis
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Univalence Principle
              %strong 305
              (2025) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.06275"}> arXiv:2102.06275
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/memo-305-1541"} ams:memo-305-1541
          %p Strict categories are also discussed in section 9.6 of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Univalent+Foundations+Project"}> Univalent Foundations Project
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Type+Theory+--+Univalent+Foundations+of+Mathematics"} Homotopy Type Theory – Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org/book/"} web
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://hottheory.files.wordpress.com/2013/03/hott-online-323-g28e4374.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The category of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Aczel"} Peter Aczel
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Gratzer"}> Daniel Gratzer
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%A5kon+Gylterud"} Håkon Gylterud
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+M%C3%B6rtberg"} Anders Mörtberg
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elisabeth+Stenholm"} Elisabeth Stenholm
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Category of Iterative Sets in Homotopy Type Theory and Univalent Foundations
              %strong 34
              Special Issue 9:
              %em Advances in Homotopy type theory
              (2024) 945-970 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129524000288"}> doi:10.1017/S0960129524000288
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04893"} arXiv:2402.04893
        .revisedby
